  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Minor coastal flooding remains possible for the next\nfew days, mainly during the daytime high tides along the lower Cape\nFear River.\n\nPositive tidal departures continue to improve, but high\nastronomical tides may still lead to minor coastal flooding the\nnext few days, mainly during the daytime high tides along the \nlower Cape Fear River. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Warming trend continues through Saturday, \nallowing temperatures to soar above seasonal norms. Next earnest\nrain chances return over the weekend.\n\nRidging will continue to build through Saturday, keeping the \nwarming trend around. Temperatures warm up a degree or two \nevery day through Friday. Highs around 90F appear possible \nalong and west of I-95 as early as Wednesday, with those chances\nsolidifying more by Thursday and Friday (could potentially be \n90-92F in some spots). Moisture parameters pick up as well, with\ndewpoints soaring into the 60s, and even into the lower 70s for\nthe coastal areas Thursday through Saturday. Friday in \nparticular could be the most hot and humid day of the week, with\nthe heat index climbing up towards 93-95 in some spots (in \nOctober, mind you).\n\nPattern starts to flip by Saturday. The heat ridge starts to\npush offshore, while a very broad trough pattern that stretches\nfrom near the PNW all the way to New England helps to suppress\nthe ridge southward. Weak shortwave energy breaks off the broad\ntrough and will help produce scattered showers and perhaps a few\nrumbles of thunder Saturday, but nothing appears to be a washout\nat this time. These rain chances appear to get a bit more\nbullish by Sunday and into early next week, as part of the \nbroad trough in the Ohio Valley amplifies and sweeps through \nthe Southeast as a more organized frontal system. Still much to \ndebate about the timing and organization of this system, along \nwith what kind of rainfall amounts to expect.\n\nWhat is becoming more apparent is a significant cooldown coming\nearly next week, where highs may only get into the 70s. Bring\nback a more legitimate fall!",
